1) TL;DR

Introduce an explicit `FocusTarget` enum (`Editor`, `Sidebar`, `Modal`) stored in `UiState`, drive `KeyContext` from that, and flip it on mouse/keyboard events. Sidebar keyboard handlers should check this explicit focus instead of deriving focus from selection. Escape in the sidebar sets focus back to editor without abusing selection.

---

2) Recommended approach (simple path) — effort: M (1–3h)

### 2.1 Define a central `FocusTarget` enum and store it in `UiState`

In `ui.rs`:

```rust
// Above UiState
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum FocusTarget {
    Editor,
    Sidebar,
    Modal,
}
```

Add to `UiState`:

```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct UiState {
    // ...existing fields...
    pub sidebar_resize: Option<SidebarResizeState>,

    /// Which top-level UI region currently has keyboard focus
    pub focus: FocusTarget,
}
```

Initialize it in constructors:

```rust
impl UiState {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            status_message: String::new(),
            status_bar: StatusBar::new(),
            transient_message: None,
            cursor_visible: true,
            last_cursor_blink: Instant::now(),
            is_loading: false,
            is_saving: false,
            active_modal: None,
            last_command_palette: None,
            drop_state: DropState::default(),
            splitter_drag: None,
            sidebar_resize: None,
            focus: FocusTarget::Editor,
        }
    }

    pub fn with_status(message: impl Into<String>) -> Self {
        Self {
            status_message: message.into(),
            status_bar: StatusBar::new(),
            transient_message: None,
            cursor_visible: true,
            last_cursor_blink: Instant::now(),
            is_loading: false,
            is_saving: false,
            active_modal: None,
            last_command_palette: None,
            drop_state: DropState::default(),
            splitter_drag: None,
            sidebar_resize: None,
            focus: FocusTarget::Editor,
        }
    }
}
```

Update modal helpers to keep focus and modal state in sync:

```rust
impl UiState {
    pub fn has_modal(&self) -> bool {
        self.active_modal.is_some()
    }

    pub fn open_modal(&mut self, state: ModalState) {
        self.active_modal = Some(state);
        self.focus = FocusTarget::Modal;
    }

    pub fn close_modal(&mut self) {
        self.active_modal = None;
        // Simplest rule: after closing a modal we always return focus to editor
        self.focus = FocusTarget::Editor;
    }
}
```

(If you later want “return to previous focus”, you can store a `previous_focus` but that’s not needed now.)

### 2.2 Make `KeyContext` read *only* from `FocusTarget`

In `app.rs`, import `FocusTarget` and update `get_key_context`:

```rust
use token::model::ui::FocusTarget; // near other uses

fn get_key_context(&self) -> KeyContext {
    let focus = self.model.ui.focus;

    KeyContext {
        has_selection: !self.model.editor().active_selection().is_empty(),
        has_multiple_cursors: self.model.editor().has_multiple_cursors(),
        modal_active: self.model.ui.has_modal(),
        editor_focused: matches!(focus, FocusTarget::Editor),
        sidebar_focused: matches!(focus, FocusTarget::Sidebar),
    }
}
```

This removes the hack:

```rust
// DELETE the old focus inference:
let sidebar_focused = self
    .model
    .workspace
    .as_ref()
    .map(|ws| ws.sidebar_visible && ws.selected_item.is_some())
    .unwrap_or(false);
```

Now keybindings with `EditorFocused` / `SidebarFocused` conditions will be based on true focus, not selection.

### 2.3 Sidebar keyboard routing: use focus, not selection

In `input.rs`, import `FocusTarget`:

```rust
use token::model::ui::FocusTarget;
```

Replace `is_sidebar_focused`:

```rust
/// Check if the sidebar file tree is focused
fn is_sidebar_focused(model: &AppModel) -> bool {
    matches!(model.ui.focus, FocusTarget::Sidebar)
}
```

Update the Escape behavior in `handle_sidebar_key` to change focus, *not* selection:

```rust
// Escape: return focus to editor
Key::Named(NamedKey::Escape) => {
    model.ui.focus = FocusTarget::Editor;
    Some(Cmd::Redraw)
}
```

If you still want ESC to clear selection as well, you can add:

```rust
if let Some(workspace) = &mut model.workspace {
    workspace.selected_item = None;
}
```

but functionally it’s not required for focus management.

The rest of `handle_sidebar_key` can stay the same; all its navigation is now gated behind “sidebar is focused” instead of “sidebar has a selection”.

### 2.4 Mouse: clicking sidebar gives sidebar focus

In `app.rs`, inside `WindowEvent::MouseInput { .. }` → `Pressed` → left mouse, in the sidebar click section:

```rust
// Existing computation of sidebar_click_info...
let sidebar_click_info = if let Some(workspace) = &self.model.workspace {
    if workspace.sidebar_visible {
        // ... hit-testing code ...
    } else { None }
} else { None };

if let Some((path, is_dir, clicked_row, clicked_on_chevron)) = sidebar_click_info {
    // NEW: any click inside the sidebar transfers focus to the sidebar
    self.model.ui.focus = FocusTarget::Sidebar;

    if path.as_os_str().is_empty() {
        // Click in sidebar but not on an item
        return Some(Cmd::Redraw);
    }

    // ...existing chevron / click / double-click logic...
}
```

This decouples “has selection” from “has focus”: you can click an empty part of the sidebar, which will give the sidebar focus even though `selected_item` might remain unchanged.

### 2.5 Mouse: clicks outside sidebar default focus to editor

Right now, if the click is not in a modal or the sidebar, it falls through to splitters, tab bars, editor hit-testing, etc.

After the modal branch and *before* sidebar hit-testing (or just after if you prefer), set a default focus target of `Editor` for any click that isn’t inside a modal:

```rust
WindowEvent::MouseInput {
    state: ElementState::Pressed,
    button: MouseButton::Left,
    ..
} => {
    if let Some((x, y)) = self.mouse_position {
        // Modal handling (unchanged)
        if self.model.ui.has_modal() {
            // existing in/outside modal handling; note both return early
            // so nothing below runs while a modal is active
            ...
        }

        // At this point, no modal is active. Default click focus = editor.
        self.model.ui.focus = FocusTarget::Editor;

        // Sidebar resize hit-test (unchanged)
        // Sidebar click handling: will override focus to Sidebar (see 2.4)
        // Splitter / group tab / editor hit-testing (unchanged)
    }
}
```

Because sidebar clicks return earlier and explicitly set `FocusTarget::Sidebar`, they override this default. Everything else (splitter drags, tab clicks, editor text area clicks, status bar) leaves focus on the editor, which is a reasonable default.

This satisfies:

- “Click on sidebar should transfer focus to sidebar”
- “Click on editor area should transfer focus to editor” (and more generally, any non-sidebar area).

### 2.6 Modal vs. focus: keep current behavior, but make it explicit

You already route keys to modals via:

```rust
if model.ui.has_modal() {
    return handle_modal_key(...);
}
```

That’s fine; with the changes above:

- `UiState::open_modal` sets `focus = FocusTarget::Modal;`
- `UiState::close_modal` sets `focus = FocusTarget::Editor;`
- `KeyContext.modal_active` and `editor_focused` are consistent with that.

You don’t need to change the modal key handling itself; just rely on `UiState` to keep focus & modal state in sync.

### 2.7 CSV cell editing stays as a “sub-focus” of the editor

CSV input already bypasses the editor via:

```rust
if is_csv_editing(model) {
    return handle_csv_edit_key(...);
}
```

You don’t need to represent CSV editing in `FocusTarget` unless you want CSV-specific keymap conditions later. For now, treat it as an override inside the “Editor” focus regime:

- `FocusTarget::Editor` + `is_csv_editing(model)` → route to CSV editor.
- `FocusTarget::Sidebar` → route to sidebar.
- `FocusTarget::Modal` or `has_modal()` → route to modal.

---

3) Rationale and trade-offs

- **Explicit focus is simpler and correct**: focus is a single, global concept (`FocusTarget`), instead of being inferred from selection, visibility, or ad-hoc checks.
- **Keeps Elm-ish structure** but doesn’t overcomplicate: we add one enum and one field in `UiState`, and reuse existing routing functions.
- **Minimal changes where behavior is wrong**:
  - `KeyContext` now reflects explicit focus.
  - `is_sidebar_focused` no longer conflates “has selection” with “has focus”.
  - Mouse click behavior now consistently updates focus.
- **Avoids large pipeline refactor**: we don’t rewire keymap vs. `handle_key` ordering; existing keymap logic remains, but can now rely on correct context flags.

Alternative (heavier) design would be to centralize *all* key routing in a single dispatcher (focus → route → keymap/editor/etc.). That’s more invasive and not necessary yet.

---

4) Risks and guardrails

- **Keybindings that ignore focus conditions**: if your keymap has bindings that don’t specify `EditorFocused` / `SidebarFocused`, they may still fire regardless of focus.
  - Guardrail: for any editor-only keys (arrows, text navigation), add an `EditorFocused` condition to the keymap. For sidebar-specific keys, add `SidebarFocused`.
- **Modal consistency bugs**: if any code manipulates `active_modal` directly instead of using `UiState::open_modal` / `close_modal`, `focus` could get out of sync.
  - Guardrail: consolidate modal open/close through those helpers; avoid setting `active_modal` directly.
- **Sidebar hidden while focused**: if the sidebar is focused and you hide it, focus still says `Sidebar`.
  - Guardrail: in the handler that hides the sidebar (`WorkspaceMsg::ToggleSidebar` or similar), if `focus == Sidebar`, set it back to `Editor`.

---

5) When to consider the advanced path

Revisit the design if:

- You introduce more focusable regions (status bar commands, embedded terminals, search panels, etc.).
- You want keymaps that are fully declarative with focus-based routing (e.g., different bindings per region without manual routing in `handle_key`).
- You need “focus stacks” (e.g., nested widgets where ESC pops back to a previous focus target, not always the editor).

---

6) Optional advanced path (outline only)

If/when you need more, evolve to:

- `FocusTarget` with more variants (e.g., `StatusBar`, `Terminal`, `EditorGroup(GroupId)`).
- A small `FocusManager` on `UiState`:

  ```rust
  pub struct FocusManager {
      current: FocusTarget,
      previous: Option<FocusTarget>,
  }
  ```

  with methods like `set(FocusTarget)`, `restore_previous()`, `is(T)`, etc.

- A unified key dispatcher that *first* checks `FocusTarget` and routes:
  - `Modal` → modal handler (no keymap).
  - `Sidebar` → sidebar handler; if not consumed, optionally keymap with `SidebarFocused`.
  - `Editor` → keymap + editor fallback / CSV.

For now, the simpler explicit `FocusTarget` + UI updates on mouse/keyboard is enough to fix your current issues and gives you a clean base to extend later.
